(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-abnormal-cell-growth-quiz"]={"title":"Abnormal Cell Growth and Differentiation Quiz 1 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is metaplasia, and what provokes it?","choices":["Disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape and loss of architectural orientation","Enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge under increased demand","Autonomous growth of an abnormal mass of tissue derived from a single genetically altered cell","Change of one cell type to another under chronic irritation or injury"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it involves a change in differentiation. The example on the slide is bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does aplasia differ from agenesis?","choices":["The organ develops only partially, leaving a functional deficiency","The organ enlarges because its individual cells enlarge","The primordial tissue exists but fails to develop into the mature organ","The primordial tissue never forms, so the organ is completely absent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — in agenesis the primordial tissue never formed at all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the four grades of neoplastic differentiation, from most to least resemblance?","choices":["Anaplasia, poorly differentiated, moderately differentiated, well differentiated","In situ, microinvasive, invasive, metastatic","Stage I, stage II, stage III, stage IV","Well differentiated, moderately differentiated, poorly differentiated, anaplasia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — anaplasia is the complete lack of differentiation. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are mesenchymal tumours derived from, and what tissues does that include?","choices":["Haematopoietic tissue: bone marrow, lymph nodes, spleen and thymus","Neural tissue: peripheral nerves, nerve sheaths and the central nervous system","Supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone","Epithelial surfaces: skin, gastrointestinal lining, respiratory lining and glandular ducts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same origin gives sarcomas when the tumour is malignant. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is an adenoma?","choices":["A malignant epithelial tumour with a glandular growth pattern","A benign mesenchymal tumour derived from connective tissue","A benign epithelial tumour producing finger-like projections from a surface","A benign epithelial tumour with a glandular pattern, or from a gland"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it sometimes secretes the hormone or hormones produced by its gland of origin. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which properties define a stem cell?","choices":["A limited life-span and a high rate of proliferation once committed to differentiation","Autonomous growth without physiologic stimuli, derived from a single genetic alteration","Unlimited capacity for self-renewal and cellular immortality, but a relatively low rate of proliferation","Unlimited capacity for self-renewal together with a very high rate of proliferation at all times"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it can also differentiate into the mature cells that give the organ its function. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What do Mx, M0 and M1 mean?","choices":["Mx, no distant metastasis; M0, metastasis cannot be assessed; M1, regional nodal metastasis","Mx, multiple metastases; M0, one metastasis; M1, no metastasis","Mx, metastasis of unknown primary; M0, metastasis to one organ; M1, metastasis to several","Mx, distant metastasis cannot be assessed; M0, no distant metastasis; M1, distant metastasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same x-and-0 convention as the nodal category. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What determines where a cancer metastasizes, according to the deck?","choices":["Chance alone, since circulating tumour cells lodge wherever the vessel first narrows","The size of the primary lesion and the duration of the disease","The histological grade of the tumour and its rate of mitosis","Venous blood flow, tumour and endothelial receptors, and genetic fitness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deck states explicitly that metastatic spread is NOT random but determined by these three. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why is dysplasia described as precancerous?","choices":["It may progress to cancer","It has already invaded through the basement membrane","It always metastasizes if left untreated for long enough","It arises from a single genetically altered cell and grows autonomously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck states this directly. Dysplasia is disordered growth, not yet malignancy. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why do cancers spreading through the portal vein and the inferior vena cava reach the liver and the lungs?","choices":["Because lymphatic drainage from most organs terminates in the liver and lungs","Because those are the organs each vein drains into","Because liver and lung tissue are uniquely receptive to any tumour cell","Because tumour cells preferentially enter arterial rather than venous blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the portal vein delivers to the liver, the inferior vena cava to the right heart and then the lungs. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Besides cervical cancer, which malignancies is high-risk human papillomavirus associated with?","choices":["Anal, vulvar, vaginal, penile and oropharyngeal cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma and gastric adenocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and certain B cell lymphomas","Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — all five are listed alongside cervical cancer, the oropharyngeal one being a squamous cell carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What do the human papillomavirus proteins E6 and E7 each block?","choices":["E6 blocks p53; E7 blocks retinoblastoma protein","E6 blocks retinoblastoma protein, which inhibits cell growth; E7 blocks p53, which promotes apoptosis","E6 blocks BRCA-1 and E7 blocks BRCA-2, both of which repair damaged deoxyribonucleic acid","E6 and E7 both block p53, doubling the loss of apoptosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — p53 promotes self destruction of mutated cells and retinoblastoma protein inhibits cell growth, so one removes apoptosis and the other removes a growth brake. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is a metastasis?","choices":["A benign nodule that develops in the tissue draining a cancer","A portion of a cancer that has migrated from the primary site to other sites","A second, unrelated primary cancer arising in another organ","Local invasion of a cancer into the tissue immediately surrounding it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the primary remains where it began; the metastasis is what has left. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is a papilloma?","choices":["A malignant tumour arising from supportive, mesenchymal tissue","A malignant epithelial tumour showing squamous cell differentiation","A tumour with finger-like or warty projections from an epithelial surface","A tumour forming a glandular pattern or arising from a gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the projections are visible microscopically or macroscopically. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What happens in a patient with normal immune function who is infected with Epstein Barr virus?","choices":["They develop nasopharyngeal carcinoma within a few years of infection","They clear the virus completely and cannot be reinfected","No immortalization; they are asymptomatic or get mononucleosis","Their B lymphocytes are immortalized, but the resulting lymphoma regresses spontaneously"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a self-limited infectious mononucleosis. Normal immune function is what stops the immortalization becoming cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is a malignant tumour of mesenchymal origin called?","choices":["A carcinoma","An adenoma","A papilloma","A sarcoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal means supportive tissue, and the malignant form is the sarcoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In which cavity does seeding most commonly occur?","choices":["The subarachnoid space","The peritoneal cavity","The pericardial cavity","The pleural cavity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pericardial and pleural cavities and the subarachnoid space are also named. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What important caveat does the deck attach to TNM definitions?","choices":["They replace histological grading, which is no longer used","They are cancer-specific; for some cancers depth of invasion matters more than size","They are identical across every cancer, which is what makes the system universal","They apply only to epithelial malignancies and not to sarcomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why the slide's example table is for one named cancer rather than all. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which carcinogens are produced by the combustion of tobacco, and what do they cause?","choices":["Aflatoxins, causing hepatocellular carcinoma","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, causing bladder and lung cancer","Aromatic amines, causing bladder and lung cancer; among the most powerful carcinogens known","Nitrosamines, causing gastric and oesophageal cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — among the most powerful carcinogens known, and tobacco smoke contains numerous others besides. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which four histological features move a tumour from the benign end towards the malignant end?","choices":["Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, and abnormal differentiation","Encapsulation, uniform nuclei, absent mitoses, and normal differentiation","Invasion, lymphatic spread, haematogenous spread, and seeding","Initiation, promotion, progression, and metastasis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the deck lays these out as a spectrum rather than a switch. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which chemical carcinogens are classically emphasised in occupational bladder cancer?","choices":["Aromatic amines","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons","Alkylating agents","Asbestos fibres"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons come from tobacco combustion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What do tumour suppressor genes do, and which examples does the deck give?","choices":["They inhibit cell growth; the examples are NF-1, NF-2, retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli","They promote regulated cell growth; the examples are growth factors and their receptors","They repair damaged deoxyribonucleic acid; the examples are BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","They trigger apoptosis in damaged cells; no specific examples are named"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — losing them removes a brake rather than adding an accelerator. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which forms of radiation does the deck name as causes of cancer?","choices":["Microwave and radiofrequency radiation","Ionizing radiation only, with no ultraviolet contribution","Ultraviolet B, and ionizing radiation","Ultraviolet A only, together with infrared radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ultraviolet B is the wavelength that damages deoxyribonucleic acid directly. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why does a tumour mass grow faster than normal tissue?","choices":["An unchanged proliferative fraction with a shorter individual cell cycle","A higher proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss","A higher proliferative fraction together with a higher rate of cell loss","A lower proliferative fraction together with a lower rate of cell loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both halves matter; it is not only that cells divide faster. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What can an adenoma sometimes do that reflects its origin?","choices":["Invade through the basement membrane into surrounding tissue","Seed the peritoneal cavity through an organ surface","Produce finger-like projections from the epithelial surface","Secrete the hormone or hormones produced by its gland of origin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the tumour retains a function of the tissue it came from. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How does the deck characterise carcinogenesis overall?","choices":["A purely inherited process, requiring a germline mutation to begin","A purely environmental process, requiring an external carcinogen","A multistep process, resulting from damage to multiple normal regulatory genes","A single-step process, resulting from one mutation in one regulatory gene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a single mutation is not enough; the damage accumulates. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which cancer patients are described as sometimes carrying an inherited mutation of BRCA-1 or BRCA-2?","choices":["All patients with xeroderma pigmentosum","A minority of breast cancer patients","The majority of breast cancer patients","All patients with familial adenomatous polyposis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the deck is careful to say a minority, not most. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What do T1 through T3 or higher indicate?","choices":["Increasing size, and increasing depth of invasion","Increasing number of regional lymph nodes involved","Increasing number of distant metastatic deposits","Decreasing degree of differentiation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and for some cancers depth of invasion matters more than size. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which abnormal interaction does the deck name as a step in invasion, besides invasion into adjacent structures?","choices":["Abnormal cell-substratum interaction","Abnormal cell-cycle checkpoint control","Abnormal apoptosis of neighbouring stromal cells","Abnormal lymphatic valve function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the cell's relationship with its own supporting matrix changes. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What do promoters do?","choices":["They cause tumour cells to detach and enter the circulation","They drive proliferation of cells already damaged by an initiator","They cause the permanent deoxyribonucleic acid damage that starts the process","They repair the deoxyribonucleic acid damage caused by initiators"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — sustained or enhanced proliferation raises the risk of successive mutations leading to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"}]};
